// ============================================================================
// MEASURES.dax — Twin Hearth Studios analytics layer
// Layered: Base (single fact) -> Date-role -> Counts/ratios -> Studio (bridge) -> Time intelligence
// One line of comment per measure states what business question it answers.
//
// FORMAT STRINGS: the four ratio measures (% of Studio Net Revenue, Net Revenue
// YoY %, Net Revenue YoY % (Like-for-like), Net Revenue YoY % (New Titles)) use
// DIVIDE and therefore return a decimal ratio — 1.2055, not 120.55. Each carries
// a Percentage format string ("0.00%") in the model, which is what renders them
// as the 120.55 / 74.21% figures printed in SCORECARD.md. The SQL proofs in
// verification/checks.json multiply by 100 to match that rendered form.
// ============================================================================

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LAYER 0 — Base measures. Single fact, order-date filter context (the default
// active relationship), no studio/bridge involvement yet.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Order Net Revenue =
SUM ( fact_order[net_usd] )
-- Gross-of-refund revenue recognised on order_date. Deliberately NOT filtered by order_status: see MODEL.md #1 — a "completed" filter would double-count the deduction on the 4,800 orders that are completed but partially refunded.

Refund Net Amount =
SUM ( fact_refund[net_refund_usd] )
-- Refund value recognised on the original order_date (Finance basis). Source column is always positive across all 7,722 rows, so this is subtracted, never added.

Net Revenue =
[Order Net Revenue] - [Refund Net Amount]
-- The company's one revenue measure: net of refunds and store cut, recognised on order date. Backs Q1, Q4, Q5, Q6, Q7 — the same measure answers all of them, only the filter context changes.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LAYER 1 — Alternate date-role measures. Both invoke the inactive
// relationship for their fact table; everything else stays on order-date by
// default so the common case (Layer 0) never has to think about them.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Cash Settled =
CALCULATE (
    [Order Net Revenue],
    USERELATIONSHIP ( fact_order[settlement_date], dim_date[date] )
)
-- What actually hit the bank in the selected period, on settlement_date — not what was sold in that period. Answers Q2.

Refunds (Treasury) =
CALCULATE (
    [Refund Net Amount],
    USERELATIONSHIP ( fact_refund[refund_date], dim_date[date] )
)
-- Cash given back in the selected period, dated on the refund itself (Treasury basis), not on the original order. Answers Q3.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LAYER 2 — Counts and ratios built on Layer 0.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Paying Players (Transacted) =
DISTINCTCOUNT ( fact_order[player_id] )
-- Anyone who placed an order in the selected period, whatever happened to it afterwards. Reporting-only footnote figure (12,807 for FY2026 Q2) — NOT the §5 denominator.

Paying Players =
COUNTROWS (
    FILTER ( dim_player, ROUND ( [Net Revenue], 4 ) > 0 )
)
-- A player counts as "paying" only if their net revenue for the period is still positive after refunds are netted off. Iterates dim_player (not fact_order[player_id]) specifically so a player whose whole order was refunded drops out of the count. ROUND(...,4) here guards the boolean test against floating-point representation noise (source data itself never carries more than 4 decimals) — it is not a display rounding and does not change the value any measure returns. Returns BLANK, not 0, for a period with no qualifying rows; DIVIDE then yields BLANK rather than a divide-by-zero. Answers Q5 (denominator) — one of the two decisions I am least sure about (see AI_WORKFLOW.md).

Revenue per Paying Player =
DIVIDE ( [Net Revenue], [Paying Players] )
-- Net revenue divided by players who still had positive net revenue after refunds. Answers Q5.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LAYER 3 — Studio measures. Everything below crosses the many-to-many
// bridge and depends on the bidirectional dim_title <-> bridge_title_studio
// relationship (MODEL.md, "the bridge direction decision").
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Company Net Revenue =
CALCULATE (
    [Net Revenue],
    ALL ( dim_studio ),
    ALL ( bridge_title_studio ),
    ALL ( dim_title )
)
-- The true company total: [Net Revenue] with every studio/bridge/title filter stripped, so it reads correctly next to a per-studio breakdown regardless of what else is on the visual. Answers Q1's company row and Q4's company row.

Studio Credit Total =
SUMX (
    VALUES ( dim_studio[studio_id] ),
    [Net Revenue]
)
-- Sum of each studio's own Net Revenue, one studio at a time — co-credited titles are deliberately re-counted once per studio that shares the credit. This is Q4's "sum of the four rows above," and it is expected to exceed Company Net Revenue by design.

Studio Total Net Revenue =
CALCULATE (
    [Net Revenue],
    ALL ( dim_title ),
    ALL ( bridge_title_studio ),
    VALUES ( dim_studio[studio_id] )
)
-- The studio total, held fixed while a visual iterates that studio's titles. Clears the title and bridge filters, then re-applies the currently selected studio with VALUES(dim_studio[studio_id]), so the studio scope is stated explicitly and never depends on filter-propagation details. Returns the total of whichever studios are in scope. With one studio in scope (the §7 case, and every studio-lead session under RLS) that is the studio's own total; with none in scope it is the company total, which is the correct reading of "share" at that grain. The many-to-many-safe denominator for Q7.

% of Studio Net Revenue =
DIVIDE ( [Net Revenue], [Studio Total Net Revenue] )
-- Each title's share of the studio total, as a ratio rendered as a percentage by its format string. Denominator is the total of whichever studios are in scope. With one studio in scope (the §7 case, and every studio-lead session under RLS) that is the studio's own total; with none in scope it is the company total, which is the correct reading of "share" at that grain. Answers Q7.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LAYER 4 — Time intelligence, built on Layer 0.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Net Revenue PY =
CALCULATE (
    [Net Revenue],
    DATEADD ( dim_date[date], -1, YEAR )
)
-- Net Revenue for the same period one calendar year earlier. Works at any grain on this calendar because every fiscal quarter is a fixed calendar-month range (Q2 is always Jan-Mar), so a one-year shift always lands on the equivalent fiscal period. Verified that the shift target is fully covered: calendar year 2025 is present in dim_date at 365/365 days.

Net Revenue YoY % =
DIVIDE ( [Net Revenue] - [Net Revenue PY], [Net Revenue PY] )
-- Growth of Net Revenue against the same period one year earlier, as a ratio (format string renders it as a percentage). Answers Q6 when the page is on a fiscal quarter.

Titles Active Both Periods =
COUNTROWS ( FILTER ( VALUES ( dim_title[title_id] ), [Net Revenue] > 0 && [Net Revenue PY] > 0 ) )
-- Number of titles with net revenue in both the selected period and the same period a year earlier. Returns BLANK, not 0, for a period with no qualifying rows; DIVIDE then yields BLANK rather than a divide-by-zero.

Net Revenue (Like-for-like) =
VAR ComparableTitles = FILTER ( VALUES ( dim_title[title_id] ), [Net Revenue] > 0 && [Net Revenue PY] > 0 )
RETURN CALCULATE ( [Net Revenue], ComparableTitles )
-- Net Revenue for only the titles that were active in both the current and prior-year period — strips out the effect of titles launching mid-comparison. Supports the Q6 growth decomposition.

Net Revenue (Like-for-like) PY =
VAR ComparableTitles = FILTER ( VALUES ( dim_title[title_id] ), [Net Revenue] > 0 && [Net Revenue PY] > 0 )
RETURN CALCULATE ( [Net Revenue PY], ComparableTitles )
-- Prior-year Net Revenue for the same title set used in Net Revenue (Like-for-like), so numerator and denominator are evaluated over an identical set of titles.

Net Revenue YoY % (Like-for-like) =
DIVIDE ( [Net Revenue (Like-for-like)] - [Net Revenue (Like-for-like) PY], [Net Revenue (Like-for-like) PY] )
-- Growth for only the titles active in both the selected period and the same period a year earlier — isolates organic growth from new-title contribution. A ratio, rendered as a percentage by its format string. Backs the Q6 footnote so that claim is measure-backed, not asserted.

Net Revenue YoY % (New Titles) =
DIVIDE ( [Net Revenue] - [Net Revenue (Like-for-like)], [Net Revenue PY] )
-- The percentage-point contribution to total YoY growth that comes from titles NOT present in the prior-year period — the numerator is their current-period revenue, but the denominator is the FULL prior-year total (same denominator as total YoY %, not the like-for-like PY figure), so this and Net Revenue YoY % (Like-for-like) are additive: their sum equals Net Revenue YoY % exactly. A ratio, rendered as a percentage by its format string. Deliberately NOT "new titles' share of current revenue" (that's a different, non-additive statistic — see MODEL.md/SCORECARD.md for the distinction).

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LAYER 5 — RLS-safe presentation measure. Depends on the Studio Lead / RLS
// design in DASHBOARD.md, not needed for the scorecard, only for the page.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Company Net Revenue (Page-Safe) =
VAR IsStudioLead =
    NOT ISEMPTY ( FILTER ( sec_user_studio, sec_user_studio[user_principal_name] = USERPRINCIPALNAME () ) )
RETURN
    IF ( IsStudioLead, BLANK (), [Company Net Revenue] )
-- ALL()/REMOVEFILTERS() never bypass row-level security, so under the Studio Lead role [Company Net Revenue] would silently return that lead's own studio total under a "Company" label. This identifies a studio lead directly from the disconnected sec_user_studio mapping table (a row exists for them) rather than by counting how many studios are still visible, and blanks the tile instead of showing a mislabelled number. The Head/CFO have no row in sec_user_studio, so they get the real company figure. Depends on sec_user_studio, the disconnected RLS mapping table defined in DASHBOARD.md — it is not part of the warehouse. See DASHBOARD.md, "The company-figure trap under RLS."
